  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 144 PM CST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nSome mid-level cloud was moving northwest across southwestern \nportions of the area early today around the back side of the \nweakening upper low over western Oklahoma. Low-level conditions \ncontinue to be dominated by Canadian high pressure centered at the \nsurface over Illinois. \n\nConditions remain benign through Monday night with upper heights \nslowly rising as the Oklahoma low continues to move to the southeast \nand surface high pressure only slowly pushes east. Winds become \nnotable Tuesday and Wednesday as an upper wave pushing northeast \nacross the Rockies and into the northern Plains brings a strong \npressure gradient. 850 mb winds of 30 to 50 knots will support \nbreezy to windy south winds, with the stronger value still expected \nin north-central Kansas. Recent NBM and LREF output suggest at least \nisolated and brief potential for Advisory-level sustained winds and \ngusts in this area but there are indications of some lower cloud \nkeeping peak mixing in check. These winds will bring slightly warmer \ntemperatures, particularly for low temperatures with better mixing \nand more cloud as lower levels moisten. The wave's trajectory to the \nnorth of the local area and still limited deep layer moisture \navailability keeps precipitation chances low, limited to late \nWednesday into Wednesday night. \n\nModified surface high pressure moves in Thursday but the next upper \nwave looks to move through Friday into Saturday. Though there \nremains to be decent model spread, this wave should move closer to \nthe area with deeper moisture to support much greater precipitation \nchances and some weak thunderstorm potential. A cold front should \npush through by Sunday for cooler temperatures after a continued mild \nlate week into the early weekend with gusty south to southwest winds \nback in place Friday and Saturday. Dewpoints well into the 50s and \nthe winds will support quite warm low temperatures for late \nNovember.",
